      "output": "Title: [FREE] The bakers at the Beverly Hills bakery baked 200 loaves of bread on Monday morning. They sold 93 loaves in - brainly.com\nURL: https://brainly.com/question/10972757\nPublished: 2018-09-06T09:30:25.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# The bakers at the Beverly Hills bakery baked 200 loaves of bread on Monday morning. They sold 93 loaves in the morning and 39 loaves in the afternoon. How many loaves of bread do they have left?\n...\nThe answer to this question is 68 loaves. To solve this, simply subtract. They start with 200 and lose 93. Now, they have 107. Then, they lose 39, leaving them with 68. I hope this helps! Could I possibly get brainliest?\n...\nThe bakers have 68 loaves of bread left after selling 93 loaves in the morning and 39 loaves in the afternoon. This is calculated by subtracting the total loaves sold from the initial amount baked. The final amount can be easily found step-by-step through basic subtraction.\n...\nTo find out how many loaves of bread the bakers have left, we need to take the initial amount of loaves and subtract the number of loaves sold throughout the day.\n...\nStart with the total number of loaves baked: The bakers baked a total of 200 loaves of bread on Monday morning.\n...\nSubtract the loaves sold in the morning: They sold 93 loaves in the morning.\n...\nSo, after the morning sales, they have 107 loaves left.\n...\nSubtract the loaves sold in the afternoon: They sold another 39 loaves in the afternoon.\n...\nTherefore, after both morning and afternoon sales, the bakers have 68 loaves of bread left.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Questions/Problem Ana runs a small bakery\nURL: https://studyx.ai/homework/104951164-questions-problem-ana-runs-a-small-bakery-she-makes-80-cupcakes-every-day-one-day-she\nPublished: 2024-09-12T11:43:08.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# Questions/Problem Ana runs a small bakery She makes 80 cupcakes every day One day, she sold 75% of the cupcakes she made and gave away 10 cupcakes to her neighbors 1 How many cupcakes did Ana sell that day? 2. How many cupcakes ware left after she sold and gave away some? 1. What is asked? 2. What are given?, 3 What operation's will be used? 4 Solution 5. How many cupcakes did Ana sell that day? 6 How many cupcakes were left after she sold and gave away some? Learners Reflection:\n...\nStep 1: Identify the ProblemWe need to find out how many cupcakes Ana sold and how many were left after selling and giving away some.\n...\n- Ana makes 80 cupcakes every day.\n- She sold 75% of the cupcakes.\n- She gave away 10 cupcakes.\n...\n- Calculate 75% of 80 to find out how many cupcakes were sold.\n- Subtract the number of cupcakes sold and given away from the total number of cupcakes made.\n...\n- 75% of 80 = 0.75 * 80 = 60 cupcakes.\n...\n- Total cupcakes made = 80.\n- Cupcakes sold = 60.\n- Cupcakes given away = 10.\n- Cupcakes left = 80 - 60 - 10 = 10 cupcakes.\n...\n1. Ana sold 60 cupcakes that day.\n2. Ana had 10 cupcakes left after selling and giving away some.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Winnie baked some muffins She sold of the\nURL: https://studyx.ai/homework/102992290-winnie-baked-some-muffins-she-sold-of-the-muffins-in-the-morning-6-of-it-in-the-afternoon\nPublished: 2024-08-17T11:12:22.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# Winnie baked some muffins. She sold of the muffins in the morning, 6 of it in the afternoon and of the remainder in the evening. Just before she closed her bakery, she sold * of the muffins that remained. If Winnie had 12 muffins left in the end, how many muffins did she bake at first?\n...\nWinnie sold a fraction, a/b, of x muffins in the morning. Remaining muffins: x(1 - a/b).\n...\nWinnie sold 6 muffins in the afternoon. Remaining muffins: x(1 - a/b) - 6.\n...\nWinnie sold a fraction, c/d, of the remaining muffins in the evening. Remaining muffins: (1 - c/d)[x(1 - a/b) - 6].\n...\nWinnie sold a fraction, e/f, of the remaining muffins. Remaining muffins: (1 - e/f)(1 - c/d)[x(1 - a/b) - 6] = 12.\n...\nAssume a/b = 1/2, c/d = 1/3, e/f = 1/4. The equation becomes: (1 - 1/4)(1 - 1/3)[x(1 - 1/2) - 6] = 12\n...\n(3/4)(2/3)[x/2 - 6] = 12(1/2)(x/2 - 6) = 12x/4 - 3 = 12\n...\nx/4 = 15x = 60\n...\nWinnie baked 60 muffins.\n\n---\n\nTitle: A bakery opened with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls.Half of the rolls were sold by noon,and 60% of the remaining rolls were sold between noon and closing time.How many dozen rolls were left unsold?\nURL: https://questions.collegedunia.com/exams/questions/a-bakery-opened-with-its-daily-supply-of-40-dozen-66a0a016f6d8f34e834cca82\nPublished: 2024-07-24T06:33:03.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nA bakery opened with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls.Half of the rolls were sold by noon,and 60% of the remaining rolls were sold between noon and closing time.How many dozen rolls were left unsold?\n...\n# A bakery opened with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls. Half of the rolls were sold by noon, and 60% of the remaining rolls were sold between noon and closing time. How many dozen rolls were left unsold?\n...\n## The Correct Option is\n...\nThe problem involves calculating the number of unsold rolls after a series of sales. Initially, the bakery starts with 40 dozen rolls. First, half of these rolls are sold by noon.\n...\n1. Calculate half of the initial supply: \\( \\frac{40}{2} = 20 \\) dozen rolls are sold by noon.\n...\n2. Determine the remaining rolls after the noon sale: The initial 40 dozen minus the 20 dozen sold equals 20 dozen remaining.\n...\n3. Between noon and closing, 60% of the remaining 20 dozen rolls are sold:\n...\n\\( 60\\% \\times 20= \\frac{60}{100} \\times 20 = 12 \\) dozen rolls.\n...\n4. Calculate the rolls left unsold by subtracting those sold in the afternoon from the remaining rolls after noon:\n...\n\\( 20 - 12 = 8 \\) dozen rolls unsold.\n...\nThe correct answer is 8 dozen rolls left unsold.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Question ID - 56594 | SaraNextGen Verified Answers\nURL: https://www.saranextgen.com/homeworkhelp/doubts.php?id=56594\nPublished: 2024-05-15T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: SaraNextGen\nHighlights:\n# Question ID - 56594 | SaraNextGen Top Answer A bakery opened with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls. Half of the rolls were sold by noon and 60% of the remaining rolls were sold between noon and closing time. How many dozen rolls were left unsold? (a)6(b)8(c)10(d)12\n...\nAnswer Key / Explanation : (d) -\n...\nNumber of rolls sold by noon = of 40 dozen = 20 dozen.\n...\nNumber of rolls sold between noon and losing time = 60% of 20 dozen\n...\n= dozen = 12 dozen.\n\n---\n\nTitle: [FREE] On Monday, Saffron bakes 324 pastries in the morning and some more in the afternoon. She sells 584 - brainly.com\nURL: https://brainly.com/question/28674347\nPublished: 2022-09-20T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nOn Monday, Saffron bakes 324 pastries in the morning and some more in the afternoon. She sells 584 pastries. At the end of the day, she has 52 pastries left. How many pastries did she bake in the afternoon?\n...\nthe number of pastries that she bake in the afternoon is equal to 312 pastries.\n...\nSaffron sells 584 pastries in the whole day and 52 pastries are left at the end of the day.\n...\nTotal pastries = 584 + 52\n...\n636\n...\nSo, the number of pastries baked in the afternoon = 636 - 324\n...\nIn afternoon = 312.\n...\nTherefore, we get that the number of pastries that she bake in the afternoon is equal to 312 pastries.\n...\nSaffron baked a total of 636 pastries during the day. After accounting for the 324 pastries she made in the morning, she baked 312 pastries in the afternoon. Thus, the number of pastries she baked in the afternoon is 312.\n...\n1. In the morning, Saffron baked a total of 324 pastries.\n2. By the end of the day, she sold 584 pastries.\n3. She had 52 pastries left at the end of the day.\n...\nNow, we can calculate the total number of pastries Saffron made throughout the day. We do this by adding the number of pastries sold to the number left at the end of the day:\n...\nTotal pastries baked= Pastries sold+ Pastries left\n...\nTotal pastries baked= 584+ 52= 636\n...\nSo, Saffron baked a total of 636 pastries in the whole day. \n\nNext, to find the number of pastries she baked in the afternoon, we subtract the number of pastries she baked in the morning from the total pastries:\n...\nPastries baked in the afternoon= Total pastries baked− Pastries baked in the morning\n...\nPastries baked in the afternoon= 636− 324= 312\n...\nTherefore, Saffron baked 312 pastries in the afternoon.\n\n---\n\nTitle: A bakery opened yesterday with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls. Half of the… - Tcs Live Preparation - Knowledge Gate AI\nURL: https://www.knowledgegate.ai/learn/TCS-LIVE-PREPARATION/numerical-ability-foundation-section/percentage/successive-percentage-change/pyq-questions?q=6960888d59af169a7037bf9a\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nA bakery opened yesterday with its daily supply of 40 dozen rolls. Half of the rolls were sold by noon and 80 % of the remaining rolls were sold between noon and closing time. How many dozen rolls had not been sold when the bakery closed yesterday?\n...\nCorrect answer: C\n...\nStep 1: Start with 40 dozen rolls. Half were sold by noon, so the remaining amount is 40 ÷ 2 = 20 dozen.\n...\nStep 2: After noon, 80% of the remaining 20 dozen were sold: 0.8 × 20 = 16 dozen sold after noon.\n...\nStep 3: Unsold when the bakery closed = remaining after noon − sold after noon = 20 − 16 = 4 dozen.\n...\nAnswer: 4 dozen rolls were not sold when the bakery closed.\n\n---\n\nTitle: A bakery had 500 sugar cookies. The list shows the number of sugar cookie..\nURL: https://askfilo.com/user-question-answers-smart-solutions/a-bakery-had-500-sugar-cookies-the-list-shows-the-number-of-3436333931383638\nPublished: 2026-02-13T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# A bakery had 500 sugar cookies. The list shows the number of sugar cookies that were sold.\n...\n- In the morning, 48 sugar cookies were sold.\n- In the afternoon, 157 sugar cookies were sold.\n- In the evening, 72 sugar cookies were sold.\n...\nHow many sugar cookies does the bakery have left?\n...\nThe bakery started with 500 sugar cookies. We need to find out how many cookies were sold in total during the day and then subtract that from the original number to find out how many are left.\n...\nAdd the number of sugar cookies sold in the morning, afternoon, and evening:\n...\n48 (morning) + 157 (afternoon) + 72 (evening) = 277\n...\nSubtract the total number of cookies sold from the original number of cookies:\n...\n500 (original) - 277 (sold) = 223\n...\nThe bakery has 223 sugar cookies left.\n...\nThe correct option is B 223.",
